#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a508c1-2379-590f-85f5-386a1990f137" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e455adf-7973-5ee4-bb3c-0bd83316580a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is no $x$-intercept" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401580} {:answer/id #uuid "d3568acd-90d7-5e24-95a2-7dddb6de4815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401581} {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401582} {:answer/id #uuid "829db562-f4d5-5e31-b45c-b397742cb7e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\pm \\sqrt{5},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401583} {:answer/id #uuid "a69ed72a-53dd-5923-8c05-9efa0f54638b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d33bd951-6cf6-508d-8421-15a11016a317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401582} :answer-field/id #uuid "d58b31e7-b6c4-5d90-9a58-b8914d59f384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba3205c4-c133-5ac7-861e-655b59a14526" :question/math-academy-id "q-16645" :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the $x$-intercept, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{1}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =9\\cdot 25 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2}+25 & =225 \\\\ 9(x+1)^{2} & =200 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{200}{9} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{200}{9}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $x$-axis at the points $(-1-\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0)$ and $(-1+\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3},0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401585 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "608bbe95-edf0-54a6-bd05-5578ba073319" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Global Maxima and Minima of a Function Whose Domain Is a Smooth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cd4c18b-e511-5b14-9f13-a5b4cb4f47f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-3$ and  ${f}_{max}=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401587} {:answer/id #uuid "2821ee27-e28a-5af2-b0bc-7da60bb40070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401588} {:answer/id #uuid "77dac977-36a1-5c53-9361-88fca32b4abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401589} {:answer/id #uuid "d746e3e5-aa5e-5fa1-88ee-69128529ad71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-1$ and  ${f}_{max}=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401590} {:answer/id #uuid "18156a3d-068d-5e69-8ef7-8771427933c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=1$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2821ee27-e28a-5af2-b0bc-7da60bb40070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${f}_{min}=-15$ and  ${f}_{max}=17$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401588} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc59060d-a9ed-5533-b31b-311227554f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e7d00266-1ded-5f4c-819a-7467528496c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167165" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2x,\\,2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2x=0 \\\\ 2y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =1-16{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 32\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\pi ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain four candidate points:\n\n$$\n(4,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(-4,0),\\,(0,-4)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(4,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(-4,0)$ | $(0,-4)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-15$ | $17$ | $-15$ | $17$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-15$ at the points $(4,0)$ and $(-4,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the points $(0,4)$ and $(0,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7948909f-8dcb-5cc0-a00f-f505a41e4004" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Region as Type I, Type II, Both, or Neither"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50c91312-6d25-5d77-a03c-b9737dcd0907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401594} {:answer/id #uuid "3070b0e6-2443-59e9-88ad-5d64b7dbe0c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401595} {:answer/id #uuid "de9e4a77-4bdb-50ab-8c39-a254aabe63cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401596} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed4ec19-9461-5c93-8e53-e713230511d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401597} {:answer/id #uuid "6572c207-612f-5e17-bdb8-cc85be4bf3b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de9e4a77-4bdb-50ab-8c39-a254aabe63cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401596} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37c6cf2-31b1-549d-9c9e-1baf99bb11bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fd6f7437-bc45-50cb-9400-bcd422c5b408" :question/math-academy-id "q-168921" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the finite region bounded between the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1?$\n\nI. It is a type I region\nII. It is a type II region\nIII. It's neither a type I nor a type II region" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region.\n\n![](images/bc/bc6b71d1fe69abd34dbd939dc62ede5201b75713e25456cf084e889bc44f449e.png)\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- The vertical line $x=0.4$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type I region.\n\n![](images/4a/4a1fd7069abaa3b3fa4d9c286773d226798bf3ad565e85663cab44557fd635c1.png)\n\n- The horizontal line $y=-1$ intersects the region in two separate intervals, as shown below. So, this can't be a type II region.\n\n![](images/26/260e8e959ca95a029a98bd4102106a66df418ea73c0640398c8fdebfdbf812f6.png)\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a316d2cb-bab1-518a-9a6c-3956bfcbcd26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401601} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec09427-50e7-5ea6-a6c5-d462ec4a977e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401602} {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401603} {:answer/id #uuid "ed368aed-25dd-5df6-b266-89719aec6636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401604} {:answer/id #uuid "d50e40c4-e8c2-5ba6-ad0e-dc0b92fad230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401603} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e70783-1025-5acd-82ba-24685ceb80f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b98dba6-8029-58a2-a81c-ae05e5ca1860" :question/math-academy-id "q-170347" :question/problem "![](images/71/71d9df0708a77639f3f7b2640ea2a03cebbfd72e6cfb3369c07d83a89eba6508.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Therefore, $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a53010-eddc-5115-8c70-0f32d0144384" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Hyperbola and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295a34e6-e842-585f-b03d-2b207719a6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401608} {:answer/id #uuid "3eac3404-fcdd-511d-b564-54a6a260657f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401609} {:answer/id #uuid "d3932dde-cf5e-5e48-9c49-40bd8d6b1366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401610} {:answer/id #uuid "809ebb14-2ff5-5591-a2dc-a7839d212e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401611} {:answer/id #uuid "dee838d7-2ccd-57a7-9289-a3238457885f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401612}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "295a34e6-e842-585f-b03d-2b207719a6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401608} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c46fcc-9e01-5325-b5ea-b258368147e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed5c021d-75c0-5eb0-8447-cc8817c3eb31" :question/math-academy-id "q-170558" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola ${x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1$ intersects the line $y=8x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=8x,$ we first replace $y$ with $8x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{(8x)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{64{x}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ -3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. So, the hyperbola does not intersect the line $y=8x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5bd766-d624-54e3-945e-7ae37b83886a" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Sentence Containing a Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc237d2e-e258-51aa-b02c-d3de373a0b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,|\\,18$ or $11$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401614} {:answer/id #uuid "95645e07-ad94-5f53-94f7-cca49c5ad899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18$ and $11$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401615} {:answer/id #uuid "d8dae576-92ac-54ed-a811-031ffd21fa2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,|\\,18$ and $11$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401616} {:answer/id #uuid "42432f20-0241-59fd-b7c8-f9f2bbb70103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,|\\,18$ and $11$ is not prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401617} {:answer/id #uuid "4e68f1f1-4450-5751-bd58-9f125f0beedf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18$ or $11$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95645e07-ad94-5f53-94f7-cca49c5ad899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18$ and $11$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401615} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c5c17af-159f-5984-9b54-f720cf946df4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "711d64e0-f4d6-5c58-a54c-d6327695bb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-171016" :question/problem "$\\,$ $3\\,|\\,18$ or $11$ is not prime.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(3\\,|\\,18\\text{ or }11\\text{ is not prime}) & ≡¬((3\\,|\\,18)∨(11\\text{ is not prime})) \\\\  & ≡¬(3\\,|\\,18)∧¬(11\\text{ is not prime}) \\\\  & ≡(3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18)∧(11\\text{ is prime}) \\\\  & ≡3\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,18\\text{ and }11\\text{ is prime}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93249d2-e5f0-5767-98da-c33b837b41c0" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfbc4514-da7a-5d32-bb0c-75cb3c1af14e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a rational number whose triple is $2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401621} {:answer/id #uuid "00b16d7a-3879-5b16-a22b-05c9128f9304" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists no rational number whose triple is $2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401622} {:answer/id #uuid "445c2f89-43cc-540c-a4c7-441ca2f3d468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a rational number whose triple is a multiple of $2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401623} {:answer/id #uuid "db52d8b1-5884-5600-886d-f29af9a8111f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Triple every rational number is not $2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401624} {:answer/id #uuid "33760338-3c64-54bf-8af4-8c4133bc6f27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Triple every rational number is $2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfbc4514-da7a-5d32-bb0c-75cb3c1af14e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a rational number whose triple is $2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401621} :answer-field/id #uuid "516df518-0f30-5fe7-9381-34b1cbb3ff17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fa1ebd9-8d46-5a04-b414-ffbaef5b882d" :question/math-academy-id "q-171998" :question/problem "$\\,$ $∃q\\in Q,\\,3q=2.$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?" :question/worked-solution "The existential quantifier \"$∃$\" means \"there exists.\" So, our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $q\\in Q$ **such that** $3q=2.$ \n\nThis can be further simplified as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a rational number whose triple is $2.$\n\n**Note:** This statement is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401626 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "875d7f2a-43ec-5635-8e72-48ae83d0339b" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Universal Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe9a0e2-c6d2-56ac-b91e-b2347fd649b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a number between $0$ and $1$ that is greater than its square." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401628} {:answer/id #uuid "286a7a4d-b364-5fd1-bc1c-cdcc0f25f9a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Consider the square of every number between $0$ and $1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401629} {:answer/id #uuid "676c24b5-3446-5b45-8adc-2f8128e722d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Every number is greater than its square." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401630} {:answer/id #uuid "db1e01e2-6988-5261-bb19-4bde540dddf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a number whose square is between $0$ and $1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401631} {:answer/id #uuid "9830dd3d-604d-5712-a275-601b3e2f9f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Every number between $0$ and $1$ is greater than its square." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9830dd3d-604d-5712-a275-601b3e2f9f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Every number between $0$ and $1$ is greater than its square." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401632} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4f005b5-9729-5d5f-b912-f7b0246c77fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "698421f5-96c5-5307-a8fb-d2ac4a250cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-171999" :question/problem "$\\,$ $∀x\\in (0,1),\\,x>{x}^{2}$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?" :question/worked-solution "The universal quantifier \"$∀$\" means \"for all.\" So, our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $x\\in (0,1),\\,x>{x}^{2}.$\n\nThis can be further simplified as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Every number between $0$ and $1$ is greater than its square.\n\n**Note:** This statement is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0beb1ecb-7649-51d5-8923-8085f1d146bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401635} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5ec049-b8d0-5c17-a28c-05813122674f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401636} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d87b1b-17a5-523c-a973-f5e0ad287cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401637} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4f4094-0cfd-5ae4-8308-88f0b83b2c27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401638} {:answer/id #uuid "946c0adc-baba-5359-a975-69b7a89f4838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa5ec049-b8d0-5c17-a28c-05813122674f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401636} :answer-field/id #uuid "5088efea-7f9e-57d5-8f9c-14c688a0e06b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "104d7814-9b21-55a2-864d-c61ec56f5c8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-17392" :question/problem "Given that $y=2{x}^{e}-ln⁡x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{e}-ln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2e\\cdot {1}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =2e-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "391bb8ae-88ba-520c-a5cf-7e2fa5474788" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Predicate Expressed in Plain English"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4342a2-aa37-5d96-b2bd-53d063965b9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-x$ is not prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401642} {:answer/id #uuid "15a35b0c-6fdf-55ef-951e-82f3c0ef6382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ is not prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401643} {:answer/id #uuid "ed204714-bf39-5405-9311-45ac92c2d622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401644} {:answer/id #uuid "ca80c410-622d-5f0f-975e-2b344e560b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401645} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b2b009-d901-5f51-ba56-c279e930d4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca80c410-622d-5f0f-975e-2b344e560b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is prime" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401645} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6698acc-7b29-5b47-adfc-142f7b407f15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "813f8661-6028-5444-9eb0-c5477e6d2450" :question/math-academy-id "q-175404" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\nP(x):x\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ prime},\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following represents $¬P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n¬P(x):\\text{It is not true that }x\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ prime},\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, $x$ is prime. Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$$\n¬P(x):x\\text{ is prime}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "391bb8ae-88ba-520c-a5cf-7e2fa5474788" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Predicate Expressed in Plain English"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa68b75b-8128-5a25-b790-f2663b29914e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not a member of the set of real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401649} {:answer/id #uuid "9cec5a45-da56-5be0-a4fe-d31742e4edcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is a member of the set of integer numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401650} {:answer/id #uuid "83772d67-29e7-568c-95eb-2014c2e04d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is a member of the set of real numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401651} {:answer/id #uuid "b37340ba-173b-5278-8d7d-6e463d3106c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not a member of the set of rational numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401652} {:answer/id #uuid "428328e2-4ee1-58bf-86ab-1eac59b08650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not a member of the set of integer numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "428328e2-4ee1-58bf-86ab-1eac59b08650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is not a member of the set of integer numbers" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401653} :answer-field/id #uuid "6588a6a5-4237-5a1f-91da-7b37766d0fa5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fa22fb7-c16d-5dc4-beee-381645b3cb56" :question/math-academy-id "q-175419" :question/problem "$\\,$ $x$ is a member of the set of integer numbers.\n\nThe predicate $P(x)$ is given above. Which of following represents $¬P(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$\\,$ It is not true that $x$ is a member of the set of integer numbers.\n\nIn other words, $x$ is *not* a member of the set of integer numbers. Therefore, $¬P(x)$ means the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is **not** a member of the set of integer numbers."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78a314f5-c68e-5618-9913-6599483e5664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401655} {:answer/id #uuid "3ccfcafe-cef5-50d0-a01d-4b0b97482678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{C-3{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401656} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb9aead-e399-5915-a838-fa945173baeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{C-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401657} {:answer/id #uuid "930d61d6-ef38-5e4c-8a33-08fa25e7168c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401658} {:answer/id #uuid "447f6dfe-e7b5-5176-98bb-437118ba132e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{C-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "930d61d6-ef38-5e4c-8a33-08fa25e7168c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401658} :answer-field/id #uuid "948f7240-5694-5bc6-95db-83a3f04326f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ffe3c5eb-c32b-557c-96a7-7ea03bab3fa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-17544" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{x}\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{z}^{-1}+2{x}^{2}{z}^{-2}}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{xz}+\\frac{2x}{{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{z}{x}-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-2x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-2x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-2{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int 2{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-2{x}^{3}}{3x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3x}{C-2{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8181ba88-9e06-5281-b3b3-c20c7b029122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x-6}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401661} {:answer/id #uuid "0333c509-36d0-531c-b1e8-c0d5eb1a0e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x+7}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401662} {:answer/id #uuid "9ed26247-cff8-52e1-b593-ea1aea2edbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x+4}{4x-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401663} {:answer/id #uuid "cf88b680-b1c2-5eb0-a109-817c0a9bd2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{5x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401664} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a50594-0018-55c3-84f5-62e1bd92ad8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf88b680-b1c2-5eb0-a109-817c0a9bd2a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5x}{5x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401664} :answer-field/id #uuid "e799e599-a900-5eac-8575-dfee31f5aa3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ccfb013-777e-5e8a-8a81-f7a1a7ca50df" :question/math-academy-id "q-17559" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y(y-1)}{x}=0,\\,y(1)=5,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{x+C}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{x+C}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition  $y(1)=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{1}{1+C}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{(x-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{(5x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "637030d2-d58f-56af-bf16-f33f755b65f9" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Plain English Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15927e61-d24a-5e9e-8234-3a83c7489a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ is not smaller than $3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401668} {:answer/id #uuid "cf6ec547-f205-5bfa-8b82-3ce3fb9cc66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ is not smaller than $5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401669} {:answer/id #uuid "60c6346a-02a6-5963-9190-7f70562c480e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ is smaller than $3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401670} {:answer/id #uuid "68d51cc8-d409-5837-9199-4a007d311037" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ is smaller than $5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401671} {:answer/id #uuid "a3306971-ca93-5299-ab72-c4d506a635f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ is equal to $5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf6ec547-f205-5bfa-8b82-3ce3fb9cc66f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ is not smaller than $5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401669} :answer-field/id #uuid "3836e3be-ceaa-5182-be58-3dca86925d41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd8d390f-c42d-56c3-b2d4-b1b0454599e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-176051" :question/problem "Given the statement\n\n$$\nP:3\\text{ is smaller than }5\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following represents $¬P?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:\\text{It is not true that }3\\text{ is smaller than }5.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, $3$ is *not* smaller than $5.$ Therefore, $¬P$ means the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:3\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ smaller than }5.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that $P$ is **true,** while $¬P$ is **false.**"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "637030d2-d58f-56af-bf16-f33f755b65f9" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Plain English Statement"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d826904-96a2-59ce-b5fc-60b5526a1ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  is not greater than  $0.1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401675} {:answer/id #uuid "390a4ec3-5b8a-5b98-bc89-d674abef5a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.1$  is not greater than $0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401676} {:answer/id #uuid "b53fd129-b8e8-5685-a1f6-07230f89a1dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  is equal to $0.1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401677} {:answer/id #uuid "252daeb4-b00c-5b00-87cc-5e54a6b731b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.1$  is greater than $0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401678} {:answer/id #uuid "778d6c76-109a-5c81-a30f-11950a9705cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  is greater than  $0.1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "778d6c76-109a-5c81-a30f-11950a9705cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$  is greater than  $0.1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401679} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4cf313f-d1b6-5abf-b5ce-f86b65e36b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0f8781c-d297-5aca-886f-46748c33ed7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-176859" :question/problem "Given the statement\n\n$$\nP:0\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ greater than }0.1\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following represents $¬P?$" :question/worked-solution "The symbol \"$¬$\" represents negation. So, our task is to negate the given sentence. \n\nTo negate our plain English sentence, we place the words \"It is not true that\" in front of it. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:\\text{It is }\\text{not}\\text{ true that }0\\text{ is }\\text{not}\\text{ greater than }0.1,\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, $0$ is greater than $0.1.$ Therefore, $¬P$ means the following:\n\n$$\n¬P:0\\text{ is greater than }0.1.\n$$\n\nNote that $P$ is **true,** while $¬P$ is **false.**"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f674fcf-7f3e-53c0-8759-6f83b0820160" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Conjunction and Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce66c39b-76e7-547b-8560-3f8586f07d61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational or less than $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401682} {:answer/id #uuid "15d749ae-772a-5eae-be65-5432afc6b914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is neither rational nor less than $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401683} {:answer/id #uuid "4d40f6cc-c5d5-5763-a712-bf7b4204ab5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401684} {:answer/id #uuid "bbda7dd0-e8b9-50bc-83b4-ebc0a9a52b4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational but not less than $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401685} {:answer/id #uuid "d69d874a-89d2-5882-9667-c0ab124288a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational and less than $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d69d874a-89d2-5882-9667-c0ab124288a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is rational and less than $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401686} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbf0b017-304e-58e5-9a9f-333116d74094" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c55e65c-37f4-5fad-8b00-23ee3e7e21f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-177758" :question/problem "Identify the sentence that is equivalent to the following: \"$x$ is rational, and $x$ is less than $2$ and it is rational.\"" :question/worked-solution "Using the commutative law, we can replace the phrase \"$x$ is less than $2$ and it is rational\" with the phrase \"$x$ is rational and it is less than $2.$\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is rational, and $x$ is rational and it is less than $2.$\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma:\n\n\"$x$ is rational and $x$ is rational, and it is less than $2.$\"\n\nNow, we can simplify the phrase \"$x$ is rational and $x$ is rational\" to just \"$x$ is rational.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is rational, and it is less than $2.$\"\n\nFinally, we can write the sentence more concisely:\n\n\"$x$ is rational and less than $2.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f674fcf-7f3e-53c0-8759-6f83b0820160" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Conjunction and Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af6d814d-d90e-5c43-8ac7-1f04325df5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even and it divides $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401689} {:answer/id #uuid "be275bd8-9162-5a47-b7ff-a74c4eba977a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401690} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac2210d-f557-57b4-a31d-f558e998356a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even or it divides $11$ but not both" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401691} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d46acf-4fcc-5090-b717-8a09704de071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ divides $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401692} {:answer/id #uuid "47d58c80-0a73-52c8-8da1-4b57f78ac610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even or it divides $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d58c80-0a73-52c8-8da1-4b57f78ac610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ is even or it divides $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401693} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d67412-b89b-5efb-ae9d-97880811f38f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fb50cec6-7c0e-563f-899c-48d469d6d9ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-177760" :question/problem "Simplify the following sentence: $x$ is even and it is an even number, or $x$ is even or it divides $11.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the phrase \"$x$ is even and it is an even number\" to just \"$x$ is even.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is even, or $x$ is even or it divides $11.$\"\n\nThen, using the associative law, we can move the comma in the sentence:\n\n\"$x$ is even or $x$ is even, or it divides $11.$\"\n\nNow, we simplify the phrase \"$x$ is even or $x$ is even\" to just \"$x$ is even.\" The sentence now reads\n\n\"$x$ is even, or it divides $11.$\"\n\nFinally, we simplify the sentence to\n\n\"$x$ is even or it divides $11.$\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa65a73-e750-5052-9249-7c096297ae51" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Minor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b9eaaa1-9a11-5e97-894b-033fdcba3183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-1$ and $k=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401696} {:answer/id #uuid "13957928-5c88-587e-8787-1039aba9a145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401697} {:answer/id #uuid "23eb6ae8-6643-5983-b5dd-d782a53c5d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401698} {:answer/id #uuid "daaad303-eb94-54dc-b583-e74bb50bf666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=0$ and $k=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401699} {:answer/id #uuid "01dc8965-c2ae-5ff0-b5e5-19a4d4675483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=10$ and $k=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b9eaaa1-9a11-5e97-894b-033fdcba3183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$k=-1$ and $k=-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401696} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6e33e7-dd66-569c-9ddd-b087772632c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a62d5fa-3b38-507f-b4fe-dfd19836563a" :question/math-academy-id "q-18244" :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, find all the possible values of $k$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2k & -8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 11 & 9 & k+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and second column:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2k & ∗ & 6 \\\\ ∗ & 5 & ∗ \\\\ 11 & ∗ & k+3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${a}_{22}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}2k & 6 \\\\ 11 & k+3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2k\\cdot (k+3)-11\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${a}_{22}$ is $-70$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{k}^{2}+6k-66 & =-70 \\\\ 2{k}^{2}+6k+4 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2}+3k+2 & =0 \\\\ (k+1)(k+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-1$ and $k=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9de08e2-7bf6-5ab5-bb90-7f2896dda1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Sentence Containing a Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "938c3ee2-cabf-5d4b-a82f-51aa90a79cbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|=0$ and $x$ is rational" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401703} {:answer/id #uuid "21140988-0342-5bc8-9482-5fe6d5552b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|\\ne 0$ and $x$ is not rational" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401704} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a791d9-ddce-54ec-9234-65d0e6facd4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|=0$ and $x$ is not rational" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401705} {:answer/id #uuid "03742fb6-2d4b-59f2-bd41-766fe48f2e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|=0$ or $x$ is not rational" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401706} {:answer/id #uuid "5eaf2475-ccba-521f-83c3-2e8cf83dcaf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|\\ne 0$ or $x$ is not rational" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03742fb6-2d4b-59f2-bd41-766fe48f2e3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$|{1,x}|=0$ or $x$ is not rational" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401706} :answer-field/id #uuid "f122c311-1c07-5ab1-ab5b-dbf45c9499a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "770c39e2-d9cc-5e78-8420-5f83d2044c16" :question/math-academy-id "q-182445" :question/problem "$\\,$ $|{1,x}|\\ne 0$ and $x$ is rational\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law posits that the negation of a conjunction of two statements is equivalent to the disjunction of the negated individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(|{1,x}|\\ne 0\\text{ and }x\\text{ is rational}) & ≡¬((|{1,x}|\\ne 0)∧(x\\text{ is rational})) \\\\  & ≡¬(|{1,x}|\\ne 0)∨¬(x\\text{ is rational}) \\\\  & ≡(|{1,x}|=0)∨(x\\text{ is not rational}) \\\\  & ≡|{1,x}|=0\\text{ or }x\\text{ is not rational}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9de08e2-7bf6-5ab5-bb90-7f2896dda1dc" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Sentence Containing a Conjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c90be5-6c78-54a6-bb74-ebd1f944ea8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is not a rhombus or $Q$ is not a rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401710} {:answer/id #uuid "82f495e5-01e7-5682-b038-4ef3a1fbc031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus or $Q$ is a rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401711} {:answer/id #uuid "096ba793-2074-5a71-9df9-d671228efdc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus and $Q$ is a rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401712} {:answer/id #uuid "1453d588-2b2c-5246-9c90-dbcc5f01a18d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus or $Q$ is not a rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401713} {:answer/id #uuid "b753b5df-38f2-5a86-8a5e-993702bfa764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus and $Q$ is not a rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82f495e5-01e7-5682-b038-4ef3a1fbc031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ is a rhombus or $Q$ is a rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401711} :answer-field/id #uuid "a74c8461-811a-581d-b088-83dfb56b4446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20f8df14-2c7c-56d3-bb1c-84da27501882" :question/math-academy-id "q-182446" :question/problem "$\\,$ $P$ is not a rhombus and $Q$ is not a rectangle.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law posits that the negation of a conjunction of two statements is equivalent to the disjunction of the negated individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows.\n\n$$\n¬(A∧B)=¬A∨¬B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,¬(P\\text{ is not a rhombus}\\text{ and }Q\\text{ is not a rectangle}) & ≡¬((P\\text{ is not a rhombus})∧(Q\\text{ is not a rectangle})) \\\\  & ≡¬(P\\text{ is not a rhombus})∨¬(Q\\text{ is not a rectangle}) \\\\  & ≡(P\\text{ is a rhombus})∨(Q\\text{ is a rectangle}) \\\\  & ≡P\\text{ is a rhombus}\\text{ or }Q\\text{ is a rectangle}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5bd766-d624-54e3-945e-7ae37b83886a" :knowledge-point/title "Negating a Sentence Containing a Disjunction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e98d0ca-b7a2-5170-9381-24b76f0454f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is not prime  or $2\\in Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401717} {:answer/id #uuid "b8773b7b-5555-5c11-b984-7a58534070f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is prime  and $2\\in Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401718} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a7643a-1d6b-5354-9347-6407878484a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is not prime  and $2\\in Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401719} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd83d82-9ddb-5f59-a77a-384e64a87e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is prime  or $2\\notin Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401720} {:answer/id #uuid "713e5e52-69ac-5388-8ad3-e0e16ad097ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is prime  or $2\\in Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8773b7b-5555-5c11-b984-7a58534070f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ is prime  and $2\\in Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401718} :answer-field/id #uuid "afaa16c5-b2b9-5a55-a11e-ac5052152f4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a692cde6-8afa-5365-a9a4-cb2917556510" :question/math-academy-id "q-182450" :question/problem "$\\,$ $2$ is not prime or $2\\notin Q.$\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?" :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n¬(A∨B)=¬A∧¬B\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(2\\text{ is not prime}\\text{ or }2\\notin Q) & ≡¬((2\\text{ is not prime})∨(2\\notin Q)) \\\\  & ≡¬(2\\text{ is not prime})∧¬(2\\notin Q) \\\\  & ≡(2\\text{ is prime})∧(2\\in Q) \\\\  & ≡2\\text{ is prime}\\text{ and }2\\in Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aa65a73-e750-5052-9249-7c096297ae51" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Minor"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205c22e6-8533-526d-adfc-b7173bb287ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401723} {:answer/id #uuid "78173574-1dd2-594f-94a9-485be0dead83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=4$ and $t=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401724} {:answer/id #uuid "f493d638-8bce-58ac-912a-5338bcaaef9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=-5$ and $t=-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401725} {:answer/id #uuid "ea9478e1-7ffc-5682-8c4f-871cb83d3a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=-5$ and $t=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401726} {:answer/id #uuid "98c916e5-ba01-54de-ae08-6a77aed9f1d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78173574-1dd2-594f-94a9-485be0dead83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$t=4$ and $t=5$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401724} :answer-field/id #uuid "5695dc0f-925d-5609-ad66-b2dab30bc5b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0c6851eb-a487-5113-99b9-8f1c04863168" :question/math-academy-id "q-18246" :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and third column:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 4 \\\\ -1 & 9 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${p}_{23}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}t & -8 \\\\ -1 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =t\\cdot 9-(-8)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =9t-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${p}_{23}$ is ${t}^{2}+12$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9t-8 & ={t}^{2}+12 \\\\ {t}^{2}-9t+20 & =0 \\\\ (t-5)(t-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $t=4$ and $t=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56a88b2d-31e2-5c81-b7af-10adf58dcfe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't exercise regularly, and you won't improve your health." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401730} {:answer/id #uuid "eeda8eae-691d-538c-893d-edf580428ade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401731} {:answer/id #uuid "f9371ba2-5ccf-57d1-a125-df93ceb1efdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401732} {:answer/id #uuid "7f5a4b12-3038-5307-aecf-3fc8e7806ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't exercise regularly, or you won't improve your health." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401733} {:answer/id #uuid "beb760f6-e95b-5af4-8271-f33e3673d4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you exercise regularly, or you won't improve your health." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9371ba2-5ccf-57d1-a125-df93ceb1efdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you do not exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401732} :answer-field/id #uuid "1369236f-0d85-59b3-8f09-5b7bf8330423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3eb0922-de2b-5aa7-8232-87f927107273" :question/math-academy-id "q-182507" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you exercise regularly, then you'll improve your health.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You exercise regularly.\n\n- $B:$ You will improve your health.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You do not exercise regularly.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You do not exercise regularly, or you will improve your health.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you do not exercise regularly, or you'll improve your health.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a0563d-13f2-5256-b18a-47d8b5f64041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401737} {:answer/id #uuid "f6731b6b-f0cf-55d7-87cc-7c66343c06ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, and you cannot enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401738} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b9de9c-dfe1-5e38-920b-ffe3d2c1e6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't buy a ticket, or you can enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401739} {:answer/id #uuid "62a4e17c-f6e3-5d49-ae77-c163f6da3e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't buy a ticket, and you cannot enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401740} {:answer/id #uuid "20b3068d-8214-5c62-ae9b-55f280ed1b92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, or you can enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a0563d-13f2-5256-b18a-47d8b5f64041" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401737} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f6ea998-1510-5ac2-a229-6d71f403c27f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc2aafac-bd5b-5b06-b1c9-744a025e7988" :question/math-academy-id "q-182570" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you don't buy a ticket, then you cannot enter the concert.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't buy a ticket.\n\n- $B:$ You cannot enter the concert.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You buy a ticket.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048fd58d-6161-542c-aca0-9aa3830ec64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Wear a jacket, and you won't get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401744} {:answer/id #uuid "2c28a06a-e16c-5c23-a019-7042d53b30bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you wear a jacket, then you will get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401745} {:answer/id #uuid "1568255d-6e74-51c6-b213-0baccfa15504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don’t wear a jacket, then you won't get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401746} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1a52bd-b4c3-59f6-aa7e-00e77fc5e146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don’t wear a jacket, then you will get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401747} {:answer/id #uuid "7703df10-5599-5c0e-b2b9-a7aaaab873cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you wear a jacket, then you won't get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f1a52bd-b4c3-59f6-aa7e-00e77fc5e146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don’t wear a jacket, then you will get cold." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401747} :answer-field/id #uuid "970b9ff4-c62f-56de-9540-47a07451659c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98ecba05-28ef-5ea8-9d45-86a0357f6077" :question/math-academy-id "q-182592" :question/problem "$\\,$ Wear a jacket, or you will get cold.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Wear a jacket.\n\n- $B:$ You will get cold.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't wear a jacket.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't wear a jacket, then you will get cold."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202c8237-3888-53f9-b30f-02e259152607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you water the plants, they will grow." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401750} {:answer/id #uuid "118d90f3-6d32-5afe-8e02-cbf41ba95880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't water the plants, they will grow." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401751} {:answer/id #uuid "89772d8b-75f5-5288-933b-dbba82a0628b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't water the plants, they won't grow." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401752} {:answer/id #uuid "a723dd49-b3ce-5d3b-8bbe-d9d5caf0b650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you water the plants, they won't grow." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401753} {:answer/id #uuid "69265ef0-339f-5ed1-8244-d0a8e9f42458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Water the plants, and they will grow." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "202c8237-3888-53f9-b30f-02e259152607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you water the plants, they will grow." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401750} :answer-field/id #uuid "95a3fcff-39ee-51ea-961b-901f1fa75b71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c37bd0c3-9779-5d09-a1bb-d6c4e0fab0c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-182595" :question/problem "$\\,$ Either you do not water the plants, or they will grow.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You do not water the plants.\n\n- $B:$ The plants will grow.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You water the plants.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, then they will grow.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ If you water the plants, they will grow.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c0b4ea8-c305-52fb-b738-39b52cd65224" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\notin N,$ or $x$ is not positive." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401757} {:answer/id #uuid "0f89fefa-c3c4-5941-854f-f243eb5a7db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\in N,$ and $x$ is positive." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401758} {:answer/id #uuid "fa092300-5642-58f4-a90f-6ea2281b0263" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\in N,$ or $x$ is positive." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401759} {:answer/id #uuid "064a345b-f5c1-5e19-82b0-253407d0787b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\notin N,$ and $x$ is positive." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401760} {:answer/id #uuid "54036453-852b-5e55-ab90-eafbae198c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\notin N,$ or $x$ is positive." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54036453-852b-5e55-ab90-eafbae198c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x\\notin N,$ or $x$ is positive." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401761} :answer-field/id #uuid "75c6802b-9187-56f8-8a12-dde53fdaa099" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d8f44ce-e231-5920-980e-3c7663e37fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-183349" :question/problem "$\\,$ If $x\\in N,$ then  $x$ is positive.\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x\\in N.$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is positive.\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x\\notin N.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x\\notin N,$ or $x$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f58690c6-735d-5579-a228-58b5fdedcdab" :knowledge-point/title "Negating Universal Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81cd1e65-4f9b-54b2-b6b0-e90a5052ee66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $t\\in Q$ such that $t<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401764} {:answer/id #uuid "58924096-406f-5237-89e1-91e281ef716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $t\\in Q$ such that $t\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401765} {:answer/id #uuid "8d041320-296d-5ba1-9fc8-c4862754bf6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401766} {:answer/id #uuid "bf93f7c8-8f44-5fe2-86fb-e73b3d7712a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $t\\in Q,$ $t<1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401767} {:answer/id #uuid "ce711d41-3a67-5509-82bc-c7e212d267b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $t\\in Q,$ $t\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58924096-406f-5237-89e1-91e281ef716f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $t\\in Q$ such that $t\\ge 1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401765} :answer-field/id #uuid "57eaefa3-f6a7-50a6-968d-23bf7fb1f0aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "030c3e29-d504-53d7-8a01-ca59114f9c38" :question/math-academy-id "q-183452" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ For all $t\\in Q,$ $t<1.$" :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(t):t<1,\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀t\\in Q,\\,P(t).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n¬(∀t\\in Q,\\,P(t))≡∃t\\in Q,\\,¬P(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, $∃t\\in Q,\\,¬P(t)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $t\\in Q$ such that $t\\ge 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f58690c6-735d-5579-a228-58b5fdedcdab" :knowledge-point/title "Negating Universal Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9706d3-878e-5f5a-8d71-1f8c29f93104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401771} {:answer/id #uuid "9d92cd18-3e84-5f45-ac52-3e75689e1424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some prime numbers are divisible by $5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401772} {:answer/id #uuid "102af750-9b07-5a1d-88e3-59388e6a3dcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some prime numbers are not divisible by $5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401773} {:answer/id #uuid "511f29ba-6dd5-5d0f-881a-33e6cf9c983e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All prime numbers are not divisible by $5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401774} {:answer/id #uuid "ffff6c41-3a54-56aa-b5dd-2fff15e337fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "All prime numbers are divisible by $5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d92cd18-3e84-5f45-ac52-3e75689e1424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Some prime numbers are divisible by $5.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401772} :answer-field/id #uuid "0352b364-ca54-5ac4-b327-306591f5283f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d99ed4b-1e64-50ce-9a89-e8a256193ea9" :question/math-academy-id "q-183463" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ All prime numbers are not divisible by $5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $P$ be the set of prime numbers. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all $x\\in P,$ $x$ is not divisible by $5.$\n\nIntroducing the universal quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $∀x\\in P,\\,x\\text{ is not divisible by }5.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(∀x\\in P,\\,x\\text{ is not divisible by }5.) & ≡∃x\\in P,\\,¬(x\\text{ is not divisible by }5.) \\\\  & ≡∃x\\in P,\\,x\\text{ is divisible by }5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ There exists $x\\in P$ such that $x$ is divisible by $5.$\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ Some prime numbers are divisible by $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb609a7e-467e-5003-b4c4-2a1637977443" :knowledge-point/title "Negating Existential Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57d31c79-99cb-527d-9b34-2a7dcf7d4269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $n\\in N$ such that $\\frac{n}{10}\\in N.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401778} {:answer/id #uuid "d1430d2c-821a-5588-9653-ab0e459b688b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $n\\in N,$ $\\frac{n}{10}\\notin N.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401779} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d17dc4-c4c3-507c-a879-cb1df4136b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $n\\in N$ such that $\\frac{n}{10}\\notin N.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401780} {:answer/id #uuid "647dcb52-a68e-5b8f-9cfc-7730d5fdb6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401781} {:answer/id #uuid "aea50b23-830d-5f10-9d25-173f5ad0d82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $n\\in N,$ $\\frac{n}{10}\\in N.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1430d2c-821a-5588-9653-ab0e459b688b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $n\\in N,$ $\\frac{n}{10}\\notin N.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401779} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed16752d-c309-52b1-87e1-9ddbf2957675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "58bb6ca7-db5e-5a82-931b-128a7693b5ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-183467" :question/problem "Negate the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ There exists $n\\in N$ such that $\\frac{n}{10}\\in N.$" :question/worked-solution "If we denote\n\n$$\nP(n):\\frac{n}{10}\\in N,\n$$\n\nour statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).\n$$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n¬(∃n\\in N,\\,P(n))≡∀n\\in N,\\,¬P(n).\n$$\n\nFinally, $∀n\\in N,\\,¬P(n)$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $n\\in N,$ $\\frac{n}{10}\\notin N.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c93249d2-e5f0-5767-98da-c33b837b41c0" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Existential Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92013b7a-990a-5205-8cac-7e0776136a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $x\\in Q$ such that ${x}^{2}=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401785} {:answer/id #uuid "950fa403-afb4-5ef8-b103-f21d430d61d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $x\\in Q,$ ${x}^{2}=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401786} {:answer/id #uuid "abd7aa3d-2334-5e0e-9c0b-fe55f310ada0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all $x\\in Q,$ ${x}^{2}\\ne 3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401787} {:answer/id #uuid "8081b467-0c02-5669-af98-e691fb189d71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For some  $x\\in Q,$ ${x}^{2}\\ne 3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401788} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3c28fc-2e79-5ffc-919d-ed38b081ed6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $x\\in (-3,3)$ such that $x\\in Q.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92013b7a-990a-5205-8cac-7e0776136a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists $x\\in Q$ such that ${x}^{2}=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401785} :answer-field/id #uuid "44d896ba-9353-5e53-bd26-80e136208868" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8238e2be-16fc-5278-bef2-5c7c9ad7976d" :question/math-academy-id "q-183515" :question/problem "$\\,$ $S(x):$ ${x}^{2}=3$\n\nGiven the predicate above, which of the following expresses the statement $∃x\\in Q,\\,S(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "The existential quantifier \"$∃$\" means \"there exists.\" So, the statement \n$∃x\\in Q,\\,S(x)$ \nreads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $x\\in Q$ **such that** ${x}^{2}=3.$\n\n**Note:** This statement is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d463a0dd-784d-55b6-a598-60819f5330d7" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Nested Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63028878-7dd8-5666-a703-3a2244219cec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a rational number $x$ such that for all $ϵ>0,$ $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401792} {:answer/id #uuid "e32e5fd4-f7df-549f-91bc-ddfd82e7ec10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a number $x$ such that for all $ϵ,$ $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401793} {:answer/id #uuid "b112f284-46d7-52f3-b2ff-ee95f01c9a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Given any rational number $ϵ>0$ there exists a real number $x$ such that $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401794} {:answer/id #uuid "6c332157-7d64-5ef0-b617-8736954ebfc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a positive real number $ϵ$ such that for all rational numbers $x,$ $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401795} {:answer/id #uuid "9407de98-85a3-5b7e-8d73-44f5d91469ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Given any real number $ϵ>0$ there exists a rational number $x$ such that $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9407de98-85a3-5b7e-8d73-44f5d91469ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Given any real number $ϵ>0$ there exists a rational number $x$ such that $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401796} :answer-field/id #uuid "55efd161-25e4-5edd-a99d-363937149d24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "090f259e-6817-5d8c-bd8b-c0774c7e3270" :question/math-academy-id "q-183573" :question/problem "$\\,$ $∀\\,ϵ\\in {R}^{+},\\,∃\\,x\\in Q,\\,-ϵ<x<ϵ.$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?" :question/worked-solution "Our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **For all** $ϵ\\in {R}^{+},$ **there exists** $x\\in Q$ **such that** $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$ \n\nThis can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Given any real number $ϵ>0$ there exists a rational number $x$ such that $-ϵ<x<ϵ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d463a0dd-784d-55b6-a598-60819f5330d7" :knowledge-point/title "Translating Statements Involving Nested Quantifiers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6790e841-0c6a-511d-9596-104d0fba6854" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a real number $x$ such that $x+y\\ne 0$ for any real number $y.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401799} {:answer/id #uuid "87a788ca-e694-5ab2-b2c8-99b2516244ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all real numbers $x$ and $y,$ $x+y\\ne 0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401800} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecf925c-30cf-53ea-8ea6-eb003db73991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a real number $x$ such that $x+y=0$ for any real number $y.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401801} {:answer/id #uuid "24715df5-3648-57cf-a0c2-7c8757e47346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "For all real number $x$ there is another real number $y$ such that $x+y=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401802} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8dff61-a1b8-558a-96c5-06cbb8e259d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are real numbers $x$ and $y$ such that $x+y=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ecf925c-30cf-53ea-8ea6-eb003db73991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There exists a real number $x$ such that $x+y=0$ for any real number $y.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401801} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e399198-cdcc-5a53-af6f-9602b7c93771" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9724aea-cc2a-5bda-9ffa-87b9cd25835a" :question/math-academy-id "q-183599" :question/problem "$\\,$ $∃\\,x\\in R,\\,∀\\,y\\in R,\\,x+y=0$\n\nWhich of the following expresses the statement given above?" :question/worked-solution "Our quantified statement reads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $x\\in R$ **such that**, **for all** $y\\in R,\\,x+y=0$ \n\nThis can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ There exists a real number $x$ such that $x+y=0$ for any real number $y.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7578c48-cc9e-5170-8ffe-5ebee6580677" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System Containing Decimals in One Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c397f249-716c-58ab-a9bf-91bfc3f7d868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0.5,\\,b=-1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401806} {:answer/id #uuid "2dec0c3e-7449-5041-96b3-12d8ef936e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.5,\\,b=2.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401807} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc082a1-ed8d-5fbd-b12d-2a2df6063e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.2,\\,b=1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401808} {:answer/id #uuid "3da6187f-1bd3-5425-a6fa-9cc0e764d299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-1.2,\\,b=0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401809} {:answer/id #uuid "ba8550aa-5225-5217-b55a-8b4d6c0d09f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=0.2,\\,b=1.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cc082a1-ed8d-5fbd-b12d-2a2df6063e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=1.2,\\,b=1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401808} :answer-field/id #uuid "4994c6c6-b044-5fa6-ae9d-9a350ba14b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8748ba37-2d9a-5451-b497-621aa22378b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-18688" :question/problem "Find the solution of the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 \\\\ 1.5a+0.5b=2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the second equation by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 &  \\\\ 1.5a+0.5b=2.5 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}b=2a-1 \\\\ 15a+5b=25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first equation is already solved for $b:$\n\n$$\nb=2a-1\n$$\n\nNow, to find the value of $a,$ we can substitute $b=2a-1$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15a+5(2a-1) & =25 \\\\ 15a+10a-5 & =25 \\\\ 25a & =30 \\\\ a & =\\frac{30}{25} \\\\ a & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $a=1.2$ into the  first equation to get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =2(1.2)-1 \\\\  & =2.4-1 \\\\  & =1.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $a=1.2$ and $b=1.4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a34f86b-faa2-5844-9680-3f6951dc4ecf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401813} {:answer/id #uuid "63214331-a811-5bf2-8cb7-4da5c8c1d678" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{cot}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}csc⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401814} {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401815} {:answer/id #uuid "5a35c415-50b4-5005-8173-6121abbd643e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}csc⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401816} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8e226f-a28c-5b1a-b7df-6f312bfd06a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401815} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7aeb284-4f80-510f-8935-07a7e23c626b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee4e083b-fcc9-5eb5-9a27-ac52f9a97afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1913" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x) \\\\  & =(-8{x}^{3})(cot⁡x)+(-2{x}^{4})(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x+2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e41ae0ea-e83b-581f-a124-c29c127545ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401820} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddc81bc-6b74-5b86-9aa6-68b7985e21ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.94" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401821} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2d52e0-5f90-57ca-a8a9-c0f4aaadbdfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=10.03" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401822} {:answer/id #uuid "e34561e5-c74f-5726-99b2-cd744a8924ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401823} {:answer/id #uuid "eea394a2-6e5a-5394-a910-d19a50a2530e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=8.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41ae0ea-e83b-581f-a124-c29c127545ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=9.46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401820} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab640647-5232-553a-be5a-08e8539221b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59ea1544-758d-5928-bf3f-4995dd8b61f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-19299" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=2.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{2}-2t+1) \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-t}+6t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =-4{e}^{-2}+6\\cdot 2-2 \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-2}+10 \\\\  & =9.458\\,658… \\\\  & ≈9.46\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1ec1379-ce50-53c7-b04d-3380676ebeb9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at a Particular Moment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9733cf69-5d70-514e-a80d-eadad1d41489" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=6.67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401827} {:answer/id #uuid "85286b89-c2a3-56c9-a420-e9d18189a069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=8.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401828} {:answer/id #uuid "259c571e-8927-56b2-95c0-5bb1688deec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401829} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebd6114-b8d6-5140-a703-c4dd01c13274" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401830} {:answer/id #uuid "5f2eab8c-d466-5781-91a5-1f6a93c6b3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "259c571e-8927-56b2-95c0-5bb1688deec4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=4.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401829} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc005a4-0a0a-5369-9aa9-e0229fc91626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3df15b1d-5c6a-5a31-88c3-94f27234c33f" :question/math-academy-id "q-19302" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={t}^{2}-2t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=3.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t-2+\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=3,$ we substitute $t=3$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =2(3)-2+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =6-2+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\  & =4.333\\,333… \\\\  & ≈4.33\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1d3691b-a1a5-5e81-bfe2-760939975d1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401834} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebae067-1d98-5398-842f-52e5beff87e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401835} {:answer/id #uuid "1270a921-f7ad-5051-be4e-3a8d359de937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401836} {:answer/id #uuid "83d51c96-b38e-5a00-a86c-b8ab59d290d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401837} {:answer/id #uuid "dcada4a4-8b8c-5274-9b06-9d7f2171686e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcada4a4-8b8c-5274-9b06-9d7f2171686e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401838} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c042d42-f6d6-52fa-992c-350b4e969301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2001916-7ed2-504f-9a8e-d02fda09253a" :question/math-academy-id "q-19493" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{1/3}$ about $x=8$ to approximate the value of ${7}^{1/3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=8.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/3} \\\\ f(8) & ={8}^{1/3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{-2/3}=\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/3} & =2+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}(x-8)+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-8}{12}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ into the above to approximate ${7}^{1/3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{1/3} & ≈2+\\frac{7-8}{12} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7c8f4da-c782-51e0-ac51-87a89f31860d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the System of Equations Associated With an Augmented Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ddbc91f-a2bc-5c20-979a-ce7e906dc06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z=1 \\\\ 3x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401841} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1394d8-a03f-5e29-aaba-bb0176d8f59b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x-2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401842} {:answer/id #uuid "9761f4c8-5981-5fd5-88ce-d6d56601691c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401843} {:answer/id #uuid "13919b67-24a3-5f25-90aa-a16013b5b7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401844} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7be1b8-6206-50f4-9b88-856c8d8e718a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x+y+3z=-2 \\\\ x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9761f4c8-5981-5fd5-88ce-d6d56601691c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401843} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8af118b-89c1-56df-aee8-e8ea929b9eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5cb76d6-8596-56c0-bf91-5764390c6dbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-20362" :question/problem "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$, $y$, and $z,$ then, the corresponding system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ 3x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8922ab07-5ac7-5f85-bfee-c30de7f4024a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401848} {:answer/id #uuid "7b15bc55-e6d8-5c21-b55e-568e588591b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401849} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8c26cd-3204-5045-8920-160928c0068e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401850} {:answer/id #uuid "7828179c-427e-5c33-a7ae-bd0edbe05cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401851} {:answer/id #uuid "db0ee922-0122-505b-abc7-21b2699ea220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e5ec4eb-fb50-5d50-87c7-e15a073c23f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401848} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ec6f4c2-cef2-5136-bda0-046075ea56a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a11f695c-004e-57f4-b1f5-cd09434f69e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-20851" :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t},\\,y=12t+1,\\,0\\le t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=12.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{12}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}}=12\\sqrt{t}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =12\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =12\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
